  "provenanceNotes": "Primary: official 2010 Cabinet Notification No.2 Jōyō kanji table PDF from Agency for Cultural Affairs (bunka.go.jp), 常用漢字表; its text layer itself states 「本表には，字種 2136 字を掲げ」 and all 2136 head characters were located as entry headwords inside the PDF (incl. 𠮟 U+20B9F). Order of `chars` = table order (五十音順 by on-reading), verified identical to primary source flow (亜哀挨愛曖…). Secondary: TSV scraped from en.wikipedia \"List of jōyō kanji\" (repo README declares the scrape source); exactly 2136 unique chars. Tertiary: jisho.org-sourced per-char JSON files (repo 9elt/jouyou), exactly 2136. Cross-source diff: wiki vs jisho differ in exactly ONE codepoint — jisho uses 叱 (U+53F1) where the 2010 table headword is 𠮟 (U+20B9F, CJK Ext-B); the official PDF text layer contains 𠮟 as the entry headword, so this table follows the government notice. Variant-glyph tokens 辨 瓣 辯 appear next to 弁 in the PDF 字欄 and are NOT separate 字種 (excluded). All 2136 codepoints are Han; 2135 in U+4E00–U+9FFF, one member (𠮟) legitimately in Ext-B per the 2010 table — the \"must be within U+4E00–U+9FFF\" assumption is off by this single official character. codepoints are decimal integers (JSON has no hex literals). Note: 写 (写真) and 台 (台風) ARE official Jōyō members (Japanese shinjitai that coincide with Chinese-simplified codepoints), so any downstream zh-residue rule must not treat presence in this table as \"not Chinese-looking\", only as \"legal in Japanese\"."
